right baby from Barcelona  [Applause]  it is  shut up hey guys it's Peter Brown  standing in for freelance reviewer  Nathan Didem for his review of FIFA 17  being consistently good can bring its  own problems among the highlight  gameplay changes in this year's FIFA we  have better throw ins low-driven shots  and the ability to control the ball from  a long keeper kick welcomed improvements  sure but still pity the marketing guide  has to make these sound exciting maybe  that's why FIFA 17 a football game that  checks all the usual boxes you'd expect  also throws in a more glamorous and  unexpected addition the journey  [Music]  the journey is a new story based mode  centering on Alex hunter a young player  breaking into professional football what  was sad stay on your feet Alex terrible  it's built from a mix of gameplay  dialogue and cutscenes which depict  Alex's evolving relationships with his  family and teammates and ultimately  whether his talent is enough to  kick-start a dream career his jaw I'm  addict choice within the journey is in  practice pretty limited outside of the  initial opportunity to choose which  Premier League side Hunter joins once  you start leveling up you can directly  apply upgrade points to specific skills  but fixed plot points underpin the story  and they happen no matter how you're  performing on the pitch or behaving off  of it  the journey sounds the occasional  awkward dramatic note interactions  between characters can seem forced and  sometimes the locations themselves are  quiet and empty giving the scene an  unreal disconnected feel and yet for all  that the journey captures something at  existing parts of FIFA never have  despite its rough edges there are  moments here that deliver a kind of  brute emotional force sure it lacks  sophistication but the journey has  something and it succeeds in attaching  an added emotional weight to your  actions on the field but if that phone  doesn't ring  don't give up as for the rest of this  year's update FIFA 17 feels like a round  of unglamorous but welcome housekeeping  after the relatively thorough gameplay  changes of last year EA has touted the  switch to the frostbite engine and  presently wide shots of stadiums look  very pretty especially at night and with  mist hanging in the air but elsewhere  you'd be hard-pressed to spot the  differences and some locations in the  journey look a little flat in  characterless the gameplay improvements  this year are typify by much-needed  tweaks but also feel like the kind of  changes you only get around to when  there's nothing really pressing to  address  so now throw-ins can be dummied much  like a fake shot giving players a way to  move defenders around and get a better  chance of keeping possession free kicks  and penalties have been freed up so you  can choose your angle of approach and  charge your cross or shot much more like  you would in a regular game physical  play too has been refined so that  holding the left trigger down is now the  way to let the game know in attack or  defense that you want your players to  engage physically with the other team it  is a small tweak but it's one that  removes some of the mysteries  surrounding jostling while defending and  seems very useful for holding on to the  ball wall in possession finally the  inclusion of low-driven shots as an  appreciable new wrinkle to offensive  play nail the timing and you're able to  power efforts on goal low and accurately  finally ending the years-long reality  defying rule that more power must equal  more hype there's also the fact that  reality or a small slice of it at least  has been invited into career mode your  objectives and managing a club are now  more specifically tailored to the club  in question it's a relatively simple way  of removing the modes previous  one-size-fits-all feel while giving a  sense of individuality to every  playthrough with a new Club ultimate  team remains FIFA's biggest draw with EA  choosing to add rather than tinker with  a winning formula the most notable  addition our squad building challenges  which require you to construct sides  with certain conditions using only  players of two different nationalities  or with a minimum chemistry level and  then submit them for rewards it's a very  smart piece of design not only giving  you a use for the scores of bronze and  silver players you accrue over the  course of a season but also making play  of the subtle and sophisticated problem  solving that already underpins Ultimate  Team systems this year's FIFA then can  be fairly summarised as one big change  that doesn't quite pay off and a lot of  small ones that do the journey is  engaging enough but right now it's not  the reason to buy FIFA 17 that remains a  combination of still excellent  fundamental gameplay and ultimate team  put together with an improved career  mode and best-in-class presentation and  FIFA 17 is this year's strongest  football game  for the maybe something on here  the defender had to cut that out and he  did vital interception  [Applause]

